Whole-slide histopathology experiments are vulnerable to failures that occur before model fitting, including annotation ambiguity, contaminated patches, patient-level leakage, color variability, incompatible intermediate artifacts, and validation choices that leak into the test set. This work presents a reproducibility-oriented workflow for cancer segmentation in digital pathology and evaluates it on DIAGSET, a public prostate histopathology dataset. The workflow converts slide and annotation pairs into HDF5-backed patch datasets, performs manual-review-guided graph cleaning, generates patient-disjoint training, validation, and test splits, applies stain normalization at runtime from training-derived state, optionally reduces only the training split by embedding-based smart sampling, trains a fixed registry of segmentation models, optimizes a two-stream ensemble on validation patients, and evaluates the frozen recipe on held-out test patients. In the DIAGSET experiment, 419 valid image--annotation cases yielded 1,186,778 retained patches across 393 patients; the held-out test set contained 79 patients and 387,325 patches. No stain-normalization strategy was uniformly best across evaluation criteria. Ruifrok obtained the highest pixel-level micro Dice (0.8289; 95\% CI 0.7796--0.8723), Macenko obtained the highest AUROC point estimate (0.9603), and the non-normalized setting obtained the highest patient-level macro Dice (0.4744; 95\% CI 0.3759--0.5726) and positive-patient Dice (0.8020; 95\% CI 0.7253--0.8672). However, all settings produced false positives in many negative patients. The results support the workflow as an auditable experimental protocol, while showing that clinical interpretation remains limited by false-positive control and by evaluation on a single dataset.
